\tag{performance}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{rlvm-load/README.md}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted and published on 2026-11-24 in the reader, the store and the loader. Two scanner threads ran by default for inputs of 256 MiB and more when this was decided; since the evening of 2026-11-24 the default is one, chosen on cold runs of the executable with the machine as engine, where the second scanner thread took a core from eight workers on eight cores: see [the settings of a plain run](dec-EGMG). The packing is unchanged, and \code{\verb~|-X scan-threads=2~} is the setting of before.}

\p{\strong{Context.} After the load path was tuned for cache misses, the loader thread of [the export loader](design-export-loader) still did everything itself for every expression record: the reader's replay, the segment record, the sweep and the block record. The scanner threads that existed only tokenised, and two of them took the load of Mathlib from 9.2 to 9.6 s down to 7.2 to 7.5 s on the JVM. A scout had found that on exporter output four fifths of the expression records are proof-private and recognisable by their ids, and that leaving them in the file for the workers does not pay ([dec-RN77](dec-RN77)).}

\p{\strong{Decision.} The scanner threads pack. At a theorem's declaration line a scanner thread knows which records since the previous declaration lie above the type's id. For each stretch of consecutive application, lambda, Pi and let records among them it prepares the bytes as they will stand in the proof block, with every reference it can encode already in place: references inside the run as distances, references to shared terms that were finished long ago as their ids, read from a location table the loader publishes. Only the references it cannot resolve stay open. The loader thread takes such a run in place of the calls for its records, resolves the open references in file order, and copies the bytes into the block.}

\p{\strong{The invariant.} The store is word for word the store of the record-by-record path on every file, and a file that fails does so with the same exception, message and line. The scanner threads decide nothing: a run is an offer in front of the ordinary entries. The reader passes it on only if its records could not fail. The loader refuses a run whenever the record-by-record path has something to do that a run cannot express (metadata was seen, the segment limit would be reached, proof blocks are off), and it justifies linking by its own state at the declaration line, not by what the scanner thread assumed; a run packed for the wrong segment is expanded into exactly the records its calls would have written. A location that is a shared id never changes again, so a stale view only leaves more references open.}

\p{\strong{Evidence.} Identity of the store: loads with scanner threads, small chunks and runs against loads with the reader's defaults agree in every name, literal, node word, row, decoded block record and location, and in every failure, on the 230 arena files in batch and pipelined mode, 600 random exports, 150 proof-shaped files, 300 hostile ones, and Init and Std. On Init 5,010,446 of 6,137,092 expression records pass through 80,691 runs and none is expanded. The store digest of the published jars and of the candidate was identical in 14 configurations.}

\p{Time, all inside quiet windows on the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host, machine engine with its default switch set where an engine runs:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Measurement}\th{Std}\th{cedar}\th{Mathlib}}
\tr{\td{Load alone, JVM, warm: before, one thread}\td{0.92 s}\td{1.36 s}\td{9.2 to 9.6 s}}
\tr{\td{Load alone: packing, 2 scanner threads}\td{0.48 to 0.56 s}\td{0.73 s}\td{5.8 to 6.2 s}}
\tr{\td{Warm pass, JVM, 8 workers: before}\td{1.61 to 1.67 s}\td{2.06 to 2.17 s}\td{20.56 s}}
\tr{\td{Warm pass, JVM, 8 workers: packing}\td{1.64 to 1.68 s}\td{2.09 to 2.10 s}\td{21.05 s}}
\tr{\td{Loader thread beside 8 workers, steady state}\td{1.13 to 0.77 s}\td{1.70 to 1.2 s}\td{12.84 to 11.11 s, one row}}
\tr{\td{Loader thread beside 4 workers}\td{0.89 to 0.46 s}\td{1.37 to 0.78 s}\td{not measured}}
}

\ul{
\li{The load alone is 1.8 times shorter on Std and cedar and 1.6 times on Mathlib than the one-thread load, and 1.2 to 1.4 times shorter than the load with tokenising scanner threads. Mathlib's load is under its 7 s target. A third scanner thread buys nothing: the linking thread is the limit again.}
\li{The loading thread needs 0.6 times the CPU it needed on Std and 0.43 times on cedar; the two scanner threads take as much again between them.}
\li{\strong{A warm pass with 8 workers on 8 cores does not change.} Such a pass is bound by its total CPU time, 7.1 to 7.4 of 8 cores busy, of which the whole load is a tenth; the packing moves that tenth between threads and does not remove it. A later window with a faster machine jar showed the same: Std 1.24 to 1.23 s, cedar 1.66 to 1.61 s, means of two rows.}
}

\p{\strong{Why go, when the 8-worker pass does not move.} The change costs such a pass nothing measurable, keeps the store identical, and removes the loader as the bound of every pass in which checking leaves a core free. As checking gets faster the load is the next floor, and this is the cheap step toward it.}

\p{\strong{Consequences.}}

\ul{
\li{Defaults: \code{\verb~|-X scan-threads~} is 2 for inputs of at least 256 MiB and none below; mapped input is chosen automatically when scanner threads run.}
\li{What would shorten a saturated 8-worker pass is less CPU in the load as a whole, now 13 to 14 \% of a Std or cedar pass. The candidates are on the scanner threads (the tape is written twice for a window) and on the loading thread: names, promotion and the shared nodes, which number shared structures and must stay in file order.}
\li{Settled in the same work: the hint that touches the shared children of nodes about to be written is kept (faster in every pair, by 2 to 10 \%); mapped against buffered input makes no difference with one thread; 8 workers stay against 7. Not resolved: the publication interval. Not adopted: forced inlining of two reader primitives in the native image, which showed no difference.}
}

\p{Detail: the sections "Parallel packing: packed runs" and "LP2: warm measurements of the load path" of \src{rlvm-load/README.md}; "Parallel packing" in \src{rlvm-export/README.md}; "LP2" in \src{rlvm-store/README.md}; the defaults in \src{rlvm-main/README.md}. Source: [the pipeline wave](ses-CVTE).}

